{"parentUuid":"c221ce3a-43f6-4823-ac20-e2d2c7e6aae8","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01RtRzo5xUZ4DQKSQueSnGCC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QMZSyYwn7tas17NpV3gPqn","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"Mezard Parisi Zecchina survey propagation random k-SAT phase transition"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4628,"cache_read_input_tokens":20987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4628,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":48,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011Caox43XF1oSz7X9AGrdLt","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ad966695-05e3-4dcc-9247-a19858174e8d","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:26.229Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"7bff28e4-39dc-4f88-8a3c-cd5107777c46","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013AQj8MAgebAWMYTjpoYcsW","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"belief propagation packing problem rectangle marginal CP-SAT hint\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"14 BELIEF PROPAGATION {ch:BP}\",\"url\":\"https://web.stanford.edu/~montanar/RESEARCH/BOOK/partD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Advanced Modeling - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/04B_advanced_modelling.html\"},{\"title\":\"Yet Another Math Programming Consultant: 2d bin packing with Google OR-Tools CP-SAT, how to fix?\",\"url\":\"http://yetanothermathprogrammingconsultant.blogspot.com/2021/02/2d-bin-packing-with-google-or-tools-cp.html\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A SAT-based Method for Solving the Two-dimensional Strip Packing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220445013_A_SAT-based_Method_for_Solving_the_Two-dimensional_Strip_Packing_Problem\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"Exact Solution Techniques for Two-dimensional Cutting and Packing\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0377221720306111\"},{\"title\":\"Leveraging Belief Propagation, Backtrack Search, and ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/~sabhar/publications/bpminicountJ-draft.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Belief Propagation in Graphical Models\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/belief-propagation-bp\"},{\"title\":\"[2011.02303] Belief Propagation on the random $k$-SAT model\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.02303\"}]\n\nI've searched for your query and found several relevant results. Here's what the search reveals:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Belief Propagation Overview:**\nBelief Propagation is a message-passing algorithm that computes marginal distributions and MAP assignments in probabilistic graphical models, computing marginals in models such as Bayesian networks, Markov random fields, and factor graphs.\n\n**BP and Packing Problems:**\nThe search results connect several relevant domains:\n\n1. **Rectangle Packing with CP-SAT:** CP-SAT uses boolean variables indicating if a rectangle is packed or not, and interval variables representing its occupied space in the container, which are used to enforce the no-overlap constraint.\n\n2. **SAT-based Approaches for Packing:** Research has solved 2D packing problems by iteratively reducing them to satisfiability testing (SAT) problems, which look for a feasible assignment of a set of boolean variables.\n\n3. **BP for SAT Problems:** The Belief Propagation message passing algorithm approximates the partition function of the random k-SAT model well for all clause/variable densities and all inverse temperatures for which a modest absence of long-range correlations condition is satisfied.\n\n4. **BP Marginal Estimates:** A solution to the BP equations provides estimates of the marginals of each variable, which is often orders of magnitude faster than using sampling for this purpose.\n\nThe intersection of these topics—using BP to estimate marginals that could serve as hints for CP-SAT solvers in rectangle packing problems—represents a promising research direction, though the search results don't explicitly document this specific combination.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0c50f2c3-96ed-474c-b0b1-b3d2e082b231","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:31.696Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"7bff28e4-39dc-4f88-8a3c-cd5107777c46","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"c221ce3a-43f6-4823-ac20-e2d2c7e6aae8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WDUHhUzusig5K9M9P8tNGF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"libDAI PGMPy factor graph belief propagation Python tool\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Belief Propagation — pgmpy 1.0.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pgmpy.org/exact_infer/bp.html\"},{\"title\":\"Belief Propagation with Message Passing — pgmpy 1.0.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pgmpy.org/exact_infer/bp_wmp.html\"},{\"title\":\"Implementing Belief Propagation in Python\",\"url\":\"https://jessicastringham.net/2019/01/09/sum-product-message-passing/\"},{\"title\":\"Client Challenge\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/tbp/\"},{\"title\":\"Supported Data Types — 1.0.0 | pgmpy docs\",\"url\":\"https://pgmpy.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Belief Propagation over Factor Graph and its Factor Nodes from Markov Network · Issue #1020 · pgmpy/pgmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pgmpy/pgmpy/issues/1020\"},{\"title\":\"pgmpy/pgmpy/inference/ExactInference.py at dev · pgmpy/pgmpy\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pgmpy/pgmpy/blob/dev/pgmpy/inference/ExactInference.py\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - akxlr/tbp: Tensor Belief Propagation - algorithm for approximate inference in discrete graphical models\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/akxlr/tbp\"},{\"title\":\"pgmpy.inference.ExactInference — 1.0.0 | pgmpy docs\",\"url\":\"https://pgmpy.org/_modules/pgmpy/inference/ExactInference.html\"},{\"title\":\"2023-02-02 PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.04110\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I've found relevant information about libDAI, PGMPy, and belief propagation for factor graphs:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**PGMPy - Python Library for Probabilistic Graphical Models:**\n\nPGMPy is a Python library for causal and probabilistic reasoning with graphical models that covers the full workflow from learning causal graphs from data to estimating causal effects, running probabilistic inference, and simulating data from fitted models.\n\n**Belief Propagation Implementation:**\n\nPGMPy creates a Junction Tree or Clique Tree for the input probabilistic graphical model and performs calibration using belief propagation. Additionally, PGMPy has a class for performing efficient inference using Belief Propagation method on factor graphs with no loops, where the message-passing algorithm recursively parses the factor graph to propagate the model's beliefs and infer the posterior distribution, with recursion stopping when reaching an observed variable or an unobserved root/leaf variable.\n\n**libDAI Connection:**\n\nThe .dfg file format was created based on libDAI's .fg file format to represent decomposed factor graphs. This indicates that libDAI is a related tool for factor graph processing that influenced other implementations.\n\n**Related Tools:**\n\nThe search also revealed other Python tools for factor graphs: PGMPy's belief propagation uses junction tree algorithm for exact inference; however it cannot be used even for the 30-unit RBM due to slow inference speed. This has led to newer alternatives like PGMax for better performance.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"be8785a1-b279-4fd5-8b2c-b7f6d84449d3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:32.564Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c221ce3a-43f6-4823-ac20-e2d2c7e6aae8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ad966695-05e3-4dcc-9247-a19858174e8d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QMZSyYwn7tas17NpV3gPqn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Mezard Parisi Zecchina survey propagation random k-SAT phase transition\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Survey and Belief Propagation on Random K-SAT | Springer Nature Link (formerly SpringerLink)\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-540-24605-3_38\"},{\"title\":\"The backtracking survey propagation algorithm for solving random K-SAT problems | Nature Communications\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/ncomms12996\"},{\"title\":\"The Condensation Phase Transition in the Regular k-SAT Model\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX-RANDOM.2016.22\"},{\"title\":\"The Decimation Process in Random $k$-SAT | SIAM Journal on Discrete Mathematics\",\"url\":\"https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/110842867\"},{\"title\":\"Phase transitions of the typical algorithmic complexity of the random satisfiability problem studied with linear programming | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0215309\"},{\"title\":\"The Algorithmic Phase Transition of Random k-SAT for ...\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10397430\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Threshold values of Random K-SAT from the cavity method\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/12960610/Threshold_values_of_Random_K_SAT_from_the_cavity_method\"},{\"title\":\"Survey Propagation: An Algorithm for Satisﬁability\",\"url\":\"http://users.ictp.it/~zecchina/rsa.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[1310.2728] The asymptotic $k$-SAT threshold\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1310.2728\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) The backtracking survey propagation algorithm for solving random K-SAT problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/72925377/The_backtracking_survey_propagation_algorithm_for_solving_random_K_SAT_problems\"}]\n\nHere are the search results for your query about Mezard, Parisi, Zecchina, survey propagation, random k-SAT, and phase transitions:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nMézard, Parisi, and Zecchina published \"Analytic and algorithmic solution of random satisfiability problems\" in Science in 2002, which is a landmark work in this field.\n\nSurvey propagation (SP) looks at clusters with a finite fraction of backbone variables, and belief propagation guided decimation is a message passing algorithm inspired by nonrigorous statistical mechanics ideas for finding satisfying assignments.\n\nSince the early 2000s, physicists developed the cavity method to put forward precise conjectures on phase transitions in random problems, with the cavity method predicting that the satisfiability threshold in random k-SAT is 2^k ln2 - ½(1+ln 2) + εk.\n\nRandom K-satisfiability problems with K=3,4 are known to be very hard close to the SAT-UNSAT threshold, and the backtracking survey propagation algorithm can find solutions very close to the threshold in practically linear time.\n\nThe search results include numerous citations and references to these foundational works, indicating their significant impact on research into computational complexity, phase transitions in combinatorial optimization, and satisfiability problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"95cb9f9e-5239-461b-96f1-7b311f5e7f9c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:32.946Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ad966695-05e3-4dcc-9247-a19858174e8d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
