{"parentUuid":"dcf2afe6-b344-4259-9309-0c388fa3ed18","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KocM6RxvJX22MA8e7yBRye","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PGMax JAX factor graph belief propagation 2024 large scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - google-deepmind/PGMax: Loopy belief propagation for factor graphs on discrete variables in JAX\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/PGMax\"},{\"title\":\"[2202.04110] PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models and Loopy Belief Propagation in JAX\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.04110\"},{\"title\":\"PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models and Loopy Belief Propagation in JAX\",\"url\":\"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v25/23-1010.html\"},{\"title\":\"PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models and Loopy Belief Propagation in JAX | Vicarious\",\"url\":\"https://www.vicarious.com/posts/pgmax-factor-graphs-for-discrete-probabilistic-graphical-models-and-loopy-belief-propagation-in-jax/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - vicariousinc/PGMax: Loopy belief propagation for factor graphs on discrete variables, in JAX!\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/vicariousinc/PGMax\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models and Loopy Belief Propagation in JAX | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/PGMax:-Factor-Graphs-for-Discrete-Probabilistic-and-Zhou-Kumar/d42afcd122357e31a58440c8ea58130de7eb5ea5\"},{\"title\":\"2023-02-02 PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2202.04110\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) PGMax: Factor Graphs for Discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models and Loopy Belief Propagation in JAX\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/358490955_PGMax_Factor_Graphs_for_Discrete_Probabilistic_Graphical_Models_and_Loopy_Belief_Propagation_in_JAX\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - swaroopgj/PGMax: Loopy belief propagation for factor graphs on discrete variables, in JAX!\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/swaroopgj/PGMax\"},{\"title\":\"PGMax/docs/source/index.rst at master · vicariousinc/PGMax\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/vicariousinc/PGMax/blob/master/docs/source/index.rst\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about PGMax:\n\n## Overview\n\nPGMax is an open-source Python package for easily specifying discrete Probabilistic Graphical Models (PGMs) as factor graphs and automatically running efficient and scalable loopy belief propagation (LBP) in JAX.\n\n## Key Features\n\n**Factor Graphs and Hardware Acceleration:**\nPGMax supports easy specification of general factor graphs with potentially complicated topology, factor definitions, and discrete variables with a varying number of states. PGMax generates pure JAX functions implementing LBP for a given factor graph that run on modern accelerators (GPU/TPU), work with JAX transformations (e.g. vmap for processing batches of models/samples, grad for differentiating through the LBP iterative process), and can be easily used as part of a larger end-to-end differentiable system.\n\n**Performance and Scalability:**\nPGMax supports general factor graphs with tractable factors, and leverages modern accelerators like GPUs for inference, obtaining higher-quality inference results with up to three orders-of-magnitude inference time speedups compared with existing alternatives. This results in an inference engine that enables PGMax to practically scale to discrete PGMs with a large number of variables and factors.\n\n## Integration with JAX Ecosystem\n\nPGMax implements LBP as pure functions with no side effects, which means users can easily apply JAX transformations like batching and automatic differentiation, and allows PGMax to seamlessly interact with the rapidly growing JAX ecosystem.\n\nThe package was published in 2022 and is maintained as open-source software by DeepMind and Vicarious.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7d480e74-0f78-441e-8742-30ba294e1b55","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:43.343Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"dcf2afe6-b344-4259-9309-0c388fa3ed18","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"66f764fa-0ae8-40b3-a251-1bba8a39ac70","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011WbiEqtZpwsh5bTtNNnU9L","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"survey propagation industrial SAT competition why fails structured instances\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SAT-Solving: Performance Analysis of Survey Propagation and DPLL\",\"url\":\"https://q4.github.io/satcmp.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"On the Hardness of SAT with Community Structure\",\"url\":\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sseshia/pubdir/sat16-hardness.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Survey propagation: An algorithm for satisfiability\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220345624_Survey_propagation_An_algorithm_for_satisfiability\"},{\"title\":\"1 On The Unreasonable Eﬀectiveness of SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.rice.edu/~vardi/papers/SATSolvers21.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT solver - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SAT_solver\"},{\"title\":\"G2SAT: Learning to Generate SAT Formulas - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7138247/\"},{\"title\":\"On the Structure of the Boolean Satisfiability Problem: A Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366788583_On_the_Structure_of_the_Boolean_Satisfiability_Problem_A_Survey\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) On the Structure of Industrial SAT Instances\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221633253_On_the_Structure_of_Industrial_SAT_Instances\"},{\"title\":\"Relaxed Survey Propagation for The Weighted Maximum ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.3483\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) SAT-Solving: Performance Analysis of Survey Propagation and DPLL\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/6064031/SAT_Solving_Performance_Analysis_of_Survey_Propagation_and_DPLL\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about survey propagation, industrial SAT competitions, and why it fails on structured instances:\n\n## Survey Propagation Performance Characteristics\n\nCertain types of large random satisfiable instances of SAT can be solved by survey propagation (SP). However, the results reveal a critical limitation: incomplete search methods such as WalkSAT and survey propagation are more effective at solving large, randomly generated formulas, while complete solvers leveraging conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL) fare better on large structured SAT formulas that commonly arise in industrial settings.\n\n## Why Survey Propagation Fails on Structured Industrial Instances\n\nThe key insight is that industrial instances are likely to be structurally different from uniformly generated random SAT instances. Survey Propagation was specifically designed and theorized to work well on random instances near the satisfiability phase transition, but its theoretical foundations don't translate to structured problems.\n\nA crucial technical limitation is that the cavity method relies on assumptions about locally tree-like structure or on the smallness of loops; on finite-dimensional lattices (short-range models like EA) those assumptions fail and the cavity equations are only approximate. Since industrial instances have rich structure with many loops and community organization, these theoretical assumptions break down.\n\nIn contrast, CDCL solvers have proven far more effective on real-world industrial benchmarks, though the exact reasons remain an active area of research in the SAT solving community.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e2dbb317-39d6-4fc3-8ad4-8e970900ef0c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:43.377Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"66f764fa-0ae8-40b3-a251-1bba8a39ac70","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"d75b956c-181b-4369-ae7a-6737acc1674f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"4909c7d4-ca8e-4ff0-8bce-62f66c25edae","agentId":"ac494304c28cb7af7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JvEuPjvEyCb6eRBM4P5xqF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"neural diving GNN marginal CP-SAT solver hint warmstart\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"NSNet: A General Neural Probabilistic Framework for Satisfiability Problems\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2022/file/a40462acc6959034c6aa6dfb8e696415-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"[2507.01825] MILP-SAT-GNN: Yet Another Neural SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.01825\"},{\"title\":\"NSNet: A General Neural Probabilistic Framework for Satisfiability Problems | OpenReview\",\"url\":\"https://openreview.net/forum?id=f-FQE1fjPK\"},{\"title\":\"Improving CDCL SAT Solving using Graph Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2110.14053\"},{\"title\":\"GraSS: Combining Graph Neural Networks with Expert Knowledge for SAT Solver Selection\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2405.11024\"},{\"title\":\"[2110.14053] NeuroBack: Improving CDCL SAT Solving using Graph Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2110.14053\"},{\"title\":\"NeuroComb: Improving SAT Solving with Graph Neural Networks Wenxi Wang\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.ucf.edu/courses/cot6410/Spring2023/SampleTopics/NeuralNetworks/Improving%20SAT%20Solving%20with%20GNNs.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7959828/\"},{\"title\":\"NEURAL NETWORKS AND THE SATISFIABILITY PROBLEM A DISSERTATION\",\"url\":\"https://users.cs.utah.edu/~tch/notes/PSSAT/IR/SAT/SAT-NN.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing Modern SAT Solver With Machine Learning Method | Proceedings of the Great Lakes Symposium on VLSI 2025\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3716368.3735251\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about neural approaches combining GNNs with SAT and constraint satisfaction solvers, though the specific combination of \"neural diving\" with all these terms didn't return a single unified paper.\n\nHere's what I found:\n\n## Neural and GNN Approaches to SAT/Constraint Solving\n\nRecent work leverages novel GNN architectures that perform marginal inference and partition function estimation to solve SAT and #SAT respectively. Specifically, neural approaches apply marginal inference to guide the initialization of a solver.\n\n## CP-SAT and Solver Integration\n\nWhile the search results don't contain specific information about \"neural diving\" techniques with CP-SAT solver hints or warmstart in combination, they do show related approaches:\n\n- Recent work predicts phases (values) of variables and uses predictions from neural models before SAT solving starts\n- Approaches focus on performing offline one-time branch initialization using GNNs to predict backbone variables, leveraging graph representations to encode Boolean formulas\n\nThese techniques appear related to warmstart and hint mechanisms where neural networks provide initialization guidance to classical solvers before execution begins.\n\nIf you're looking for more specific information about \"neural diving\" as a particular technique or paper, you may want to check recent arXiv preprints or papers from major SAT/constraint solving conferences like SAT, CP, or IJCAI, as this appears to be an active research area.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"46f1766d-d66d-4292-8c6a-85f4aa01d154","timestamp":"2026-05-07T21:52:44.591Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d75b956c-181b-4369-ae7a-6737acc1674f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-